    "observation": "the graph returned 2 node(s) of kind \"linkedSignal\" carrying label \"agreeing\"/\"differing\" and value(s) 7/8, and 1 node(s) of kind \"computed\" carrying label \"plainComputed\" and value(s) 14",
    "finding": "a linked signal carries the label of the field it is assigned to: the 2 nodes of kind 'linkedSignal' report label \"agreeing\" and \"differing\". The runtime does not derive that label — the graph reads label: consumer.debugName (spikes/ng222-app/node_modules/@angular/core/fesm2022/_debug_node-chunk.mjs:12521, \"label: consumer.debugName,\"), the public linkedSignal copies whatever debugName option it is handed (spikes/ng222-app/node_modules/@angular/core/fesm2022/_resource-chunk.mjs:135, \"getter[SIGNAL].debugName = debugName;\"), and the Angular compiler is what hands the field name over: it rewrote each initializer in the development build to `...ngDevMode ? [{ debugName: \"agreeing\" }] : []` (agreeing = linkedSignal( () => this.source(), ...ngDevMode ? [{ debugName: \"agreeing\" }] : ( /* istanbul ignore next */ [] ) )). The plain computed in the same graph is labelled \"plainComputed\" the same way, so the label does not say \"linked signal\" — the node kind does ('linkedSignal' versus 'computed'). The source signal is reachable from the graph only as the producer both linked signals share (node 2, kind 'signal', label \"source\", value 7); the page template never reads it. The agreeing subject holds 7 and its source holds 7; the differing subject was written once to 8, so it no longer equals its source."
